A bi-objective optimization method to minimize the makespan and energy consumption on parallel SLM machines
Junjie Lin1, Chunlong Yu2*, Jianfeng Lu3

2023 IEEE 19th International Conference on Automation Science and Engineering (CASE) | 979-8-3503-2069-5/23/$31.00 ©2023 IEEE | DOI: 10.1109/CASE56687.2023.10260369

Abstract— The scheduling problem in additive manufacturing system has attracted attentions in recent years. Most concern the productivity objective, only a few have considered the scheduling impact on machine energy consumption and hence, the potential of energy-saving without sacrificing productivity is still not clear. To investigate this, we address the bi-objective scheduling problem in parallel selective laser melting (SLM) machines to minimize the total energy consumption and makespan. The nesting and scheduling decisions are jointly considered. We present a mixed integer linear programming model, and propose an epsilon constraint approach to obtain the Pareto-optimal front. The properties of the problem are investigated by numerical experiments, and some insights are provided. Keywords: 3D printing, multi-objective scheduling, energy saving, makespan
I. INTRODUCTION
As additive manufacturing (AM) has been widely applied, the scheduling in AM production has become increasingly important (Che et al., 2021). Recent studies have explored the nesting, i.e., parts are placed in a limited build platform without overlap, and scheduling problem in AM systems to minimize the makespan. Kucukkoc et al. (2018) proposed mathematical models for single-machine and multi-machine environments. Zhang et al. (2020) considered the parallel machines scheduling problem with irregular part shapes and proposed a genetic algorithm. Alicastro et al. (2021) considered a nesting and scheduling problem of unrelated parallel machines, and proposed a local search algorithm enhanced by a reinforcement learning mechanism for neighborhood selection.
However, additive manufacturing has been recognized as an energy-intensive technology, especially in large-scale parts and mass production. The formation of multiple thin layers from raw materials requires a considerable amount of energy, and thus the energy consumption per unit volume of material is often much higher than conventional manufacturing techniques (Yoon et al., 2014). This aspect raises concerns about the potential negative environmental impacts and therefore, reducing energy consumption is becoming one of the most important goals driving the adoption of additive manufacturing in key industries (Karimi et al., 2021).
Some studies focus on energy-aware scheduling. Paul et al. (2012) optimized the build orientation of the parts and layer thickness to reduce energy consumption for selective laser sintering (SLS). Karimi et al. (2021) proposed a mix integer

linear programming (MILP) model that adjusts process parameters at different printing stages and controls the starting time of each job. The model succeeds to reduce the peak power and thus the electricity cost. Piili et al. (2015) discovered that a multiple-parts build can lead to an energy reduction of 8192% compared to a single-part build. The reason is that the parts assigned to one batch can share the recoat time and heating/cooling time. Oh et al. (2020) found that the part orientation policy has impact on the batch height and the total number of batches, which, consequently, can have an impact on the energy consumption. In summary, many studies have focused on reducing the energy consumption and costs by scheduling or adjusting process parameters. Few have paid attention to the energy-saving potential brought by part nesting and build orientation selection, which are quite important for the printing efficiency and energy consumption. There is also a lack of investigation on how the energy consumption and makespan will compromise when different nesting and scheduling strategies are applied. Therefore, there rises an interesting question: would it be possible to save energy without delaying too much the maximum completion time?
In this study, we consider the nesting and scheduling problem in unrelated parallel selective laser melting (SLM) machines with the objective of minimizing the total energy consumption and makespan. The decisions of build orientation selection, part-to-batch allocation, nesting and batch-tomachine assignment are to be optimized. The contributions of this study are as follows:
1. We formulate the bi-objective nesting and scheduling problem for parallel selective laser melting (SLM) machines, and provide an efficient MILP model.
2. An ε -constraint approach is proposed to obtain the Pareto front, with which the trade-off between the makespan and energy consumption is clearly illustrated. Some managerial insights are provided.
The remaining of the paper is organized as follows. Section II describes the problem. Section III describes the energy consumption model. Section IV proposes the mathematical model. Section V introduces the ε-constraint method. Section VI reports the results, and Section VII concludes the paper.
