2) Unrelated Parallel Machines To test our model on unrelated parallel machines, we modified the instance in_20_2_1 as follows. The preheating time and recoating time of machine 1 are 2115s and 8 s/layer, while these for machine 2 are 1928s and 11s/layer, respectively. All other machine parameters are identical. This new instance is named “in_20_2_1(UR)”. It is observed from Figure 5 that all batches are allocated to machine 1 on which the recoating time is less, as a penalty, machine 2 is left idle and the makespan is much larger.
3) Comparison to Industrial Software We compare the results of Algorithm 1 to Magics on in_20_2_1, in_20_2_5, in_30_2_1 and in_30_2_5. The Magics solutions is generated as follows. Firstly, the build orientations of the parts are fixed to the one with the lowest Zaxis height. Then, the parts are batched by using the automatic nesting function of Magics. Finally, the batches are allocated to the machines in such a way that the makespan is minimized. This divide-and-conqueror methodology, i.e., parts are firstly grouped to form batches (nesting), then, the batches are assigned and sequenced on the AM machines (scheduling), inevitably leads to potential gap from the optimal joint decisions (Yu et al., 2022). Our research tackles the joint nesting-scheduling problems. As shown in Figure 6, the Magics approach is dominated by the fronts given by Algorithm 1. This reveals the need to tackle the nesting and scheduling simultaneously to get better solutions. Moreover, when there are more alternative build orientations, Algorithm 1 tends to find better schedules. Figure 7 showcases the part placement results obtained from Magics and MILP-E on in_20_2_1. While Magics demonstrates batches with relatively balanced space utilization, MILP-E effectively assigns parts with similar heights to the same batch, resulting in a reduction in the total number of layers required and overall energy consumption.

VII. CONCLUSION
In this study, we investigate the nesting and scheduling problem on parallel SLM machines to minimize the energy consumption and makespan. We consider the 2D nesting subproblem when packing the parts into batches as well as the fact that a part can have multiple optional build orientations. We propose a mathematical programming model and several improvements to strengthen the model. As shown by the numerical results, the trade-off between makespan and energy consumption is clear. There is still room for better solutions by increasing the efficiency of the model. Future works will focus on developing efficient solution approaches such as matheuristics and metaheuristics to tackle large-scale problems.
